            <ArticleTitle>[Optical coherence tomography for evaluation of neointimal proliferation after placement of a new drug eluting stent].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1063-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate neointimal proliferation following placement of a new drug-eluting stent (BUMA) by optical coherence tomography (OCT).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-two patients with coronary artery disease were randomized into BUMA group (n=15) and Endeavor group (n=7) and underwent OCT imaging after 9 months of stent implantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The neointima hyperplasia (NIH) thickness in BUMA group were significantly smaller than that in endeavor group (0.220-/+0.140 mm vs 0.269-/+0.207 mm, P&lt;0.001), and the uncovered Struts were significantly lower in BUMA group than in Endeavor group (5.65% vs 6.56%, P&lt;0.0001). The luminal late loss in BUMA group was also significantly lower (34.87-/+11.50 vs 40.82-/+18.53, P=0.025).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">BUMA stent is safe and effective for treatment of coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
